---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How to Know If Your Pool Has a Leak | GK Swimming Pools"
  description="Rising water bill? Topping off more than usual? Here is how to tell if your pool has a leak, including the bucket test and dye test you can run yourself."
  canonical="https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-leak-detection"
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": "How to Know If Your Pool Has a Leak",
    "description": "Rising water bill? Topping off more than usual? Here is how to tell if your pool has a leak, including the bucket test and dye test you can run yourself.",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-24",
    "author": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "telephone": "(818) 804-8204" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "url": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com" },
    "image": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/images/blog/pool-leak-detection-hero.webp",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-leak-detection" }
  })} />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I know if my pool has a leak?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The most common early signs are a rising water bill with no change in usage, and topping off the pool more often than you used to. To confirm it, run a bucket test: fill a bucket with pool water, set it on a step so the levels match inside and outside, mark both lines, and wait 24 hours. If the pool drops noticeably more than the bucket, you likely have a leak rather than normal evaporation."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the bucket test for a pool leak?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The bucket test compares your pool's water loss against a control. Both the bucket and the pool lose water to evaporation at roughly the same rate, so if the pool level drops significantly further than the water inside the bucket over 24 hours, the extra loss is coming from a leak."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "When should I call a professional for pool leak detection?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Call a professional once you have confirmed a leak exists but cannot see where it is coming from. Leaks in underground plumbing, the pool shell, or beneath the deck cannot be found with a dye test. Professional leak detection uses specialized equipment to pinpoint the source, which avoids unnecessary digging and guesswork."
        }
      }
    ]
  })} />

  <section class="blog-hero">
    <div class="blog-hero__bg">
      <img src="/images/blog/pool-leak-detection-hero.webp"
        alt="GK Swimming Pools technician performing pool leak detection with specialized equipment"
        fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1400" height="811" />
    </div>
    <div class="blog-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="container blog-hero__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pool Repair Guide</span>
      <h1>How to Know If Your Pool Has a Leak</h1>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span>GK Swimming Pools</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>September 24, 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-body">

        <div class="blog-intro">
          <p>A leaking pool doesn't always announce itself. Sometimes it's obvious, a visible crack or a puddle forming nearby, but often the signs are subtle enough that homeowners don't notice until the water bill shows up. Knowing what to look for, and how to test it yourself, can save you money and catch a problem before it gets worse.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Early Signs</span>
          <h2>Your Water Bill Is Rising for No Clear Reason</h2>
          <p>This is usually the first sign people notice. If your water bill has crept up and you haven't changed how you use your pool, that's worth paying attention to.</p>
          <p>Pools naturally lose some water to evaporation, but a leak loses water at a rate evaporation can't explain, and it shows up directly in your bill over a month or two.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <h2>You're Adding Water More Often Than Usual</h2>
          <p>If you've noticed yourself topping off the pool more frequently than normal, that's another early indicator. Every pool loses a small amount of water over time, but if you're adding water noticeably more often than you used to, especially outside of peak summer heat, it's a sign something beyond normal evaporation is going on.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Test It Yourself</span>
          <h2>The Bucket Test</h2>
          <p>This is the simplest way to check whether water loss is from evaporation or an actual leak.</p>

          <div class="step-list">
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">1</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Set the bucket in the pool</h3>
                <p>Fill a bucket with pool water and place it on a step in the pool, so the water level inside the bucket matches the pool's water level.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">2</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Mark both water lines</h3>
                <p>Mark the water line on both the inside and the outside of the bucket.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">3</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Wait 24 hours</h3>
                <p>Leave everything undisturbed for a full day so both levels have time to change.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">4</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Compare the two lines</h3>
                <p>Check how far each level dropped and compare them against each other.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="blog-tip">
            <div class="blog-tip__label">Reading the Result</div>
            <p>If the pool's water level dropped significantly more than the level inside the bucket, that's a strong sign you have a leak rather than normal evaporation, since both should lose water at roughly the same rate from evaporation alone.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Pinpointing It</span>
          <h2>The Dye Test</h2>
          <p>If you suspect the leak is coming from a specific spot, like a crack, a light fixture, or a fitting, a dye test can help pinpoint it.</p>
          <p>With the pump off and the water still, place a small amount of dye near the suspected area. If there's a leak, the dye will get pulled toward it as water escapes.</p>
          <p>This works best for visible cracks or suspected spots around fittings and lights, rather than leaks hidden in underground plumbing.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Next Steps</span>
          <h2>When to Call In Professional Leak Detection</h2>
          <p>The bucket and dye tests are useful for confirming that a leak exists, but they won't tell you everything, especially if the leak is in the plumbing, the pool shell, or somewhere underground.</p>
          <p>Professional pool leak detection uses specialized equipment to pinpoint exactly where water is escaping, which saves time and prevents unnecessary digging or guesswork.</p>

          <div class="callout-warn">
            <p>If you've noticed a rising water bill, you're adding water more than usual, or your bucket test confirmed a leak, it's time to get it looked at properly. The longer a leak goes untreated, the more water and money it costs, and in some cases it can lead to structural damage around the pool.</p>
          </div>

          <p>If you think your pool has a leak, give GK Swimming Pools a call. We provide pool leak detection and repair throughout the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita area, and we'll help you find the source and get it fixed.</p>

          <div class="blog-cta-inline">
            <a href="/services/pool-repairs" class="btn btn--primary">Pool Repair Services</a>
            <a href="tel:8188048204" class="btn btn--outline">(818) 804-8204</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Think You Have a Leak?</h2>
      <p>We'll find the source and tell you exactly what it takes to fix it. Serving Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get In Touch</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-hero { position:relative; height:460px; overflow:hidden; display:flex; align-items:flex-end; }
  .blog-hero__bg { position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .blog-hero__bg img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 40%; display:block; }
  .blog-hero__overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top, rgba(6,47,97,.90) 0%, rgba(6,47,97,.35) 60%, rgba(6,47,97,.12) 100%); }
  .blog-hero__inner { position:relative; z-index:2; padding-bottom: var(--space-12); }
  .blog-hero__inner .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-hero__inner h1 { color:var(--color-white); font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3.6vw,2.6rem); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); max-width:820px; }
  .blog-meta { display:flex; align-items:center; gap: var(--space-3); color:rgba(255,255,255,.72); font-size:var(--text-sm); font-weight:600; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .blog-meta__dot { width:4px; height:4px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-aqua); }

  .blog-body { max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; }
  .blog-intro { background:var(--color-mist); border-left:4px solid var(--color-aqua); padding:var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin-bottom:var(--space-12); border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0; }
  .blog-intro p { font-size:var(--text-lg); color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; line-height:1.7; margin:0; }
  .blog-section { margin-bottom:var(--space-12); }
  .blog-section .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-section h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .blog-section p { color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.85; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .blog-divider { border:none; border-top:2px solid var(--color-border); margin:var(--space-12) 0; }

  /* BUCKET TEST STEPS */
  .step-list { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--space-3); margin:var(--space-6) 0; }
  .step-item {
    display:flex; gap:var(--space-5); align-items:flex-start;
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding:var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
  }
  .step-item__num {
    flex-shrink:0; width:38px; height:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--color-aqua); color:var(--color-deep);
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:800; font-size:1.05rem;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; line-height:1;
  }
  .step-item__body h3 {
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-base);
    color:var(--color-deep); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em;
    margin-bottom:var(--space-2);
  }
  .step-item__body p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; margin:0; }

  /* WARNING CALLOUT */
  .callout-warn {
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left:3px solid #e8a33d; border-radius:var(--radius-sm);
    padding:var(--space-6); margin:var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .callout-warn p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.8; margin:0; }

  .blog-tip { background:var(--color-deep); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .blog-tip__label { font-size:var(--text-xs); font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family:var(--font-heading); }
  .blog-tip p { color:rgba(255,255,255,.88); margin:0; }

  .blog-cta-inline { display:flex; gap: var(--space-4); flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .btn--outline { display:inline-block; border:2px solid var(--color-deep); color:var(--color-deep); font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-sm); letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-8); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); text-decoration:none; }

  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .blog-hero { height:360px; }
    .blog-intro { padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5); }
    .step-item { gap:var(--space-4); padding:var(--space-4) var(--space-5); }
    .blog-cta-inline { flex-direction:column; }
  }
</style>
